{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"wideeyed-sealbrown-nag","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`.provision.log` is written into a worktree when its venv is provisioned, but it is not\nlisted in `.gitignore`. Because it is untracked-and-not-ignored, `git status --porcelain`\nis non-empty for the life of the worktree.\n\nThat is not merely cosmetic. The machine-local worktree GC\n(`com.navapbc.rebar-worktree-gc`) refuses to reclaim any worktree whose status is\nnon-empty — the \"no uncommitted work\" safety guard. A provisioning artifact therefore\npins a worktree permanently: it can never age out, and no amount of idleness makes it\ncollectable. A 2026-08-06 audit of this checkout found 6 worktrees whose ONLY untracked\nfile was `.provision.log`, plus the same guard firing on in-scope agent worktrees\n(`KEEP agent-a1bf191c510431899: dirty`).\n\nThe file is pure build/provisioning noise: it records the output of venv creation and the\neditable install. Nothing reads it after provisioning, and it is never intended to be\ncommitted.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd `.provision.log` to `.gitignore`, next to the other provisioning/rebuild noise\nentries (`.venv/`, `__pycache__/`, `.pytest_cache/`, `.ruff_cache/`) so it sits with the\nartifacts it belongs to rather than at the end of the file.\n\nAn unanchored `.provision.log` pattern is deliberate: the file is written at whatever\ndirectory the provisioner runs in, so matching it at any depth is the intended behavior,\nconsistent with how `__pycache__/` and `*.pyc` are already written in this file.\n\nThis is a one-line mechanical change to a config file. It does not alter any Python\nsource, any CLI behavior, or any gate.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `.gitignore` — add one entry.\n\nNo other file changes. No `src/rebar` change, so no test-file change is implied.\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new testable behavior is added: `.gitignore` is consumed by git itself, not by any\nrebar code path, and no test imports or executes it. Verification is therefore direct\nobservation of git's own behavior plus the repo's existing gates:\n\n- `git check-ignore -v .provision.log` exits 0 and names the new `.gitignore` line\n  (before the change it exits 1).\n- With a `.provision.log` present in a clean worktree, `git status --porcelain` is empty\n  (before the change it reports `?? .provision.log`).\n- `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass (enforced by the pre-commit hook).\n- CI's `Verified` label on the Gerrit change is `+1`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `.gitignore` contains a `.provision.log` entry; checked by\n      `grep -c '^\\.provision\\.log$' .gitignore` returning 1.\n- [ ] `git check-ignore -v .provision.log` exits 0 and attributes the match to\n      `.gitignore`.\n- [ ] In a clean checkout with a `.provision.log` file created, `git status --porcelain`\n      produces no output.\n- [ ] No file other than `.gitignore` is modified by the change; checked by\n      `git show --name-only` listing exactly that one path.\n- [ ] The change reaches `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1` on Gerrit and is submitted to\n      `main`.","id":"2cf5-6c0b-cdfa-4b64","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["janitor"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"gitignore: ignore .provision.log so a provisioning artifact cannot pin a worktree"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786065835949164001,"uuid":"50572f32-295a-452e-bfcd-1dcac3e8796f"}